The WO₃ microstrain term survives save/reopen replay at a finite value (~3150 µstrain) and improves Rwp from 5.62% to 5.57%; the size alternative hit its bound and was rejected. I’m now testing specimen displacement against the current zero-offset model, with all phase scales, cells, and the common profile allowed to reconverge so the comparison is fair.

The displacement trial found a nominally plausible height (~−0.59 mm) but failed the required replay test and drove the CuWO₄ size term to its hard lower bound, so that parameterization is not acceptable as-is. I’m giving it one controlled re-test with crystallite size neutralized; if it remains unstable or does not improve isolated-family positions consistently, the stable zero-offset baseline will be retained.

The controlled displacement model is replay-stable once the unsupported size term is removed, and it fits better than the constant-zero model. I’m rebuilding the common width baseline from neutral broadening now; this is the slower check because the profile must reconverge after removing both phase-strain terms, but it directly tests whether those terms are genuinely required rather than inherited.

